This is a program to generate character sheets for Dungeons & Dragons, 5th edition, with advanced automation features.

Currently hosted at https://koen.vosjes.cloud

The core of the program is an inference engine written in Prolog (rooted at `server/server.pl`). The user can interact with the inference engine directly through a Prolog interpreter (such as SWI-Prolog), or through a web interface.

To run the inference engine, install SWI-Prolog (swi-prolog.org/)

## Running with Podman or Docker

Build the image:

    podman build -t charsheet .

A prebuilt image is published to the GitHub Container Registry by GitHub Actions on every push, so building locally is optional:

    podman pull ghcr.io/koenp/charsheet:latest
    podman tag ghcr.io/koenp/charsheet:latest charsheet

Run a container:

    podman run --rm -p 8000:8000 -v charsheet-data:/app/storage charsheet

Then browse to <http://localhost:8000/>.

Character data lives in `/app/storage`, so mount a persistent volume at that path. The Elm frontend is compiled during the image build, so the committed `elm/build/charsheet.js` is not used.

## Accounts and registration

The site requires an account. Registration is open to the public and protected by
an [mCaptcha](https://mcaptcha.org/) proof-of-work challenge; logging in uses the
account's password.

- Passwords are hashed with PBKDF2-SHA512 (crypto_password_hash/3 from SWI's
  crypto library, 131072 iterations) and stored in storage/accounts.pl
  alongside the character data. There is no plain-text password anywhere.
- Verification uses the semidet form crypto_password_hash/2.
- Logins are tracked in the SWI HTTP session (`logged_in_as/1`); each account
  gets its own character namespace derived from its name, so accounts cannot see
  each other's characters.
- The captcha widget is embedded from `static/js/mcaptcha-glue.js`. It is
  `@mcaptcha/vanilla-glue@0.1.0-rc2`, sha256
  `7e4136a97b924f39b858ca3c29b9b826d66d08fc128ee5188530ba3318836b0e`, MIT OR
  Apache-2.0.

### Configuration

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `MCAPTCHA_URL` | mCaptcha instance base URL, e.g. `https://mcaptcha.example.org` |
| `MCAPTCHA_SITEKEY` | site key of this app's site in the mCaptcha instance |
| `MCAPTCHA_SECRET` | secret of that same site (server-side only) |
| `CHARSHEET_REQUIRE_CAPTCHA` | set to `false` to disable the captcha (local development only) |
| `CHARSHEET_SECURE_COOKIES` | set to `false` to drop the `Secure` flag from the session cookie (needed for plain-HTTP local development) |
| `CHARSHEET_ACCOUNTS_FILE` | account database path, default `storage/accounts.pl` |

Captcha verification **fails closed**: if the captcha is required but the
instance is unreachable or rejects the token, registration is refused. When the
three `MCAPTCHA_*` variables are unset, the registration form renders without a
captcha and registration is refused unless `CHARSHEET_REQUIRE_CAPTCHA=false` is
set explicitly.

Example with a captcha:

    podman run --rm -p 8000:8000 -v charsheet-data:/app/storage \
      -e MCAPTCHA_URL=https://mcaptcha.example.org \
      -e MCAPTCHA_SITEKEY=<sitekey> \
      -e MCAPTCHA_SECRET=<secret> \
      charsheet

### Resetting a password (manual)

`tools/reset-password.pl` administers accounts without the web interface. It must
be run from the application root:

    # list accounts
    podman exec charsheet swipl --quiet tools/reset-password.pl --list

    # set a new password (prints a generated one when --password is omitted)
    podman exec charsheet swipl --quiet tools/reset-password.pl --reset <username>

    # create an account directly (useful while captcha is enabled)
    podman exec charsheet swipl --quiet tools/reset-password.pl --create <username>

It exits non-zero on failure (unknown user, invalid name or password, bad
arguments). Run it while the service is idle: the tool and the server both
rewrite `storage/accounts.pl` wholesale, so a concurrent change can be lost.

